"""Biological unit and estimand methods, without new scientific scoring."""

def add(P):
    P.h('Measurement hierarchy and the unit of a forecast')
    P.p('A microbiome dataset has several nested units that must remain distinct: an analytical run, a biological specimen, a collection occasion, a participant, and a recruited source family. A matrix column is not automatically the unit of inference. Two sequencing runs can measure one specimen; two aliquots can share one collection; several stools can belong to one person; and two deposited studies can reuse one recruitment. The platform treats the reconstruction of this hierarchy as part of defining the task, before fitting or splitting. A method intended to generalize to a new person must hold that person out, whereas a method intended to transfer to a new recruitment or laboratory needs a source-level boundary. More rows improve measurement of the units already sampled but do not automatically add independent biological replication.')
    P.p('Compatible raw sequencing runs of one specimen can be summed before composition normalization. The implemented run-collapse routine checks the supplied run-to-sample relationships, requires all run columns to be explained, refuses a run assigned to multiple samples, and excludes identified assembly columns. It also rejects an identifier that is simultaneously a run and an assembly. These checks prevent a pooled assembly from being counted again as an independent measurement beside the reads that generated it. Compatibility of the assays, pipelines and raw count interpretation remains a source requirement. The routine does not verify that requirement and must not be used to sum unrelated abundance units merely because their columns share names.')
    P.p('A numerical example shows why the order of aggregation matters. Suppose two compatible runs from one hypothetical specimen have counts (90,10) and (1,9) for taxa A and B. Pooling raw counts gives (91,19), with relative abundance of A equal to 91/110, approximately 0.8273. Averaging the two separately normalized runs instead gives A a weight of (0.9+0.1)/2, or 0.5. The second calculation weights the ten-read run equally with the hundred-read run. Neither arithmetic rule can be selected by which later gives a better model score: the source measurement contract determines whether compatible raw reads are being pooled or two genuinely separate measurements are being averaged. These are hypothetical counts, not a new empirical result.')
    P.p('Technical concentration replicates require a different decision. Repeated injections from one extract measure analytical variation, not repeated biological exposure. Summing their concentrations would usually change the unit and cannot borrow the raw-read pooling rule. A prospectively specified summary, duplicate-quality criterion and quantitation treatment are needed before learning from such measurements. Distinct stools from the same day are also not automatically technical replicates: they can be separate specimens with real within-day variation. The source must identify which level was repeated. The SCFA development task in this paper excludes labeled quality-control duplicates rather than treating them as additional people. The independent high-fiber lead illustrates the separate need to distinguish analytical samples from its participant denominator; it has not supplied an admitted quantitative task here.')
    P.p('Repeated collection times remain valuable after this distinction. They can estimate a within-person trajectory, expose transient changes, and support a baseline-to-follow-up endpoint. They should not be shuffled across training and test as if they were unrelated people when the target is a new participant. A participant with densely collected stools also should not dominate a population endpoint merely through a larger row count unless that row-weighted target was deliberately chosen. One defensible design scores a fixed participant-level summary and then weights participants equally. Another targets a randomly chosen collection occasion and weights occasions. These answer different questions. A paper must state which population its average describes instead of allowing file layout to choose the estimand.')
    P.h('Exposure windows, source boundaries and paired comparisons')
    P.p('Time labels need the same discipline. A visit label, a collection date, a day since the first dose and a day since the last dose are different coordinates. The local course-design screen takes explicit numerical start and end times, requires a strictly earlier baseline and strictly later follow-up, and selects the nearest eligible samples around the course. Nonnegative timing margins can be added prospectively. A specimen exactly at the start or end does not qualify under those strict inequalities. The routine refuses duplicate submitted source-subject-time coordinates so that technical-replicate resolution is not hidden inside a pairing rule. It never reads outcomes, estimates an antibiotic response, or establishes that the supplied treatment dates are correct.')
    P.p('The screen also checks whether another submitted course overlaps the selected baseline-to-follow-up window. If another course falls between those observations, the default rule blocks attribution to a single isolated exposure. This is a design check rather than an adjustment model: recording an overlap does not estimate its effect or remove confounding. Unknown courses, adherence, concurrent medication, illness and dietary changes can still matter even when the submitted window passes. A nominal recovery phase cannot replace a verified treatment timeline. The Raymond distinction between ninety days after treatment end and ninety days after first dose, and the Palleja collection-versus-visit and extra-course findings, are concrete source-admission reasons to preserve the original temporal meaning rather than quietly rename the axis.')
    P.p('Source grouping addresses dependence above the participant. The lineage guard registers documented accession aliases under a shared source and refuses a train, validation or test split that places one known family in multiple partitions. An unknown accession fails rather than becoming independent by default. A separate exact-mirror screen excludes old accessions but labels all remaining records only as candidates with near-duplicate status unchecked. This asymmetry is intentional: finding a known overlap establishes a problem, while failing to find one does not establish independence. A source-family registry needs positive recruitment, sample and processing evidence. Distinct repository URLs, projects or study titles are identifiers to investigate, not independent experiments to count.')
    P.p('Evaluation aggregation then has to follow the intended boundary. The implemented study-aware comparison computes each submitted study\'s median candidate error and median comparator error, subtracts them, and averages those study differences with equal study weight. Its bootstrap resamples studies together, not individual rows from within a study. A large source cannot dominate merely because it has many specimens. Nevertheless, supplied study identifiers may still conceal repeated recruitment, related participants or laboratory dependence; the routine marks external-win certification false regardless of its interval. With fewer than two submitted studies it reports insufficient studies and does not manufacture a study-bootstrap interval. These are properties of a statistic, not permission to call a viewed development study external validation.')
    P.p('Paired losses also do not make every possible contrast equivalent. The difference between two study medians is generally different from the median of samplewise paired differences. Likewise, a mean participant loss can differ from a median taxon loss pooled over participants. These choices are not interchangeable translations of one result. The paired prediction files, sample coverage, outcome mask, weighting rule and comparator selection must all be frozen for the same task. The mouse-level sensitivities earlier in this paper retain the original forecasts and explicitly change the biological aggregation level; they do not rewrite the archived taxon-pair benchmark. A future independent comparison should specify the endpoint and resampling unit before observing whether either aggregation favors the new model.')
    P.p('Implementation anchors are src/microtwin/sample_collapse.py, src/microtwin/course_design.py, src/microtwin/subject_partition.py, src/microtwin/source_family.py, src/microtwin/mirror_guard.py and src/microtwin/leaderboard_stats.py. This method synthesis introduces no fresh fit, cohort outcome or empirical benchmark. It identifies the contracts required to turn specimen rows into a scientifically interpretable forecast and explains why the archived 160-table audit, the viewed mouse results and the first-period SCFA test retain separate scopes. The remaining external-test and perturbation-validation gates require new qualifying evidence, not a larger count of analytical rows.')
